# autostart.txt -- programs started automatically at boot, one per line.
#
# Each line is a shell command, run like in the terminal: the first word is a /bin
# tool (/bin/<word>) and the rest are its arguments. Launch a DESKTOP app with
# the `run` tool. Blank lines and '#' comments are ignored.
#
#   run voronoy   -> /bin/run voronoy  -> launches apps/voronoy.app/main
#   keyb FR       -> /bin/keyb FR      -> set the French keyboard layout
#   sleep 5       -> init builtin: wait 5 s before the next line (staggered startup)
#
# voronoy draws the generated wallpaper then exits -- optional (drop it for the plain
# blue desktop). It no longer masks the old single-GUI-app boot hang, fixed in the
# kernel (the compositor now runs as soon as it is created: CScheduler::YieldTo).
# The packages staged for this boot (a system update: docs/pkg/README.md) moved in first; it
# restarts the Pi when the kernel or the firmware changed.
wait pkg commit
run voronoy
#setup# FIRST BOOT: Setup, the first-run wizard (apps/setup), runs alone over the wallpaper. The
#setup# lines it holds back meanwhile are written "#setup: <line>"; when it ends ("Start Onyx") it
#setup# gives them back (and starts them), removes its own "run setup" line and these "#setup#"
#setup# lines, sets the keyb line and turns the remote services below on or off ("#telnetd": off).
run setup
# System menu bar across the top of the screen: the active app's menus + the clock.
#setup: run menubar
# Notification bubbles (the "notify" IPC service; apps call notify() from notify.h).
run notifyd
# The update daemon: once a day, the packages set "auto" updated, a notification for the others
# (docs/pkg/README.md; SD:/etc/pkg/pkg.ini: check = never stops it).
run pkgd
# The shared clipboard's service (its history of 10 copies: docs/clipboard/README.md)
run clipd
# The print service: the queue of the jobs the apps print (docs/03 "Printing"; the printers: the Control
# Panel's Printers applet). Started here for the jobs left in the queue when the Pi was turned off.
run printd
# The dock along the bottom (the drawers of apps, the workspaces, the Terminal, the File Viewer,
# the Trash, lock / Control Panel / power: SD:/etc/dock.ini). It replaced the Shelf (removed on 2026-10-05)
# and the panel.
#setup: run dock
#setup: run agenda
# Stickies: the pinned notes on the desktop (Notes, View menu)
#setup: run stickies
keyb FR
# Remote text shell on TCP port 23 (telnet / tools/onyx-telnet.py). NO password,
# NO encryption: remove this line on an untrusted network.
telnetd
# Remote desktop (VNC, port 5900): any VNC viewer. NO password either.
vncd
# Remote windows (port 3390): the Onyx windows on a Windows PC (pc/dist/OnyxRemote.exe).
rdpd
# Programs loaded ahead and kept in memory (large ones: they then start without reading the
# card, and their code is in memory once whatever the number of their processes): the list of
# SD:/etc/preload.ini (the Control Panel's Preload applet; one program a line -- a path, an
# app's name or a /bin tool's). Here, after the desktop's lines: the load runs in the
# background. `preload` alone lists them, `unload <program>` releases one.
preload /boot
